 \section{Human Lower-Limb Biomechanics}\label{sec:bg_biomechanics}
 
-Designing a biomimetic leg requires a quantitative benchmark of human muscle--joint behavior. This dissertation uses OpenSim \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2011, seth_opensim_2018} and the Gait2392 model, which descends from the interactive lower-extremity model of \citet{delp_interactive_1990} and includes musculoskeletal geometry from \citet{hoy_musculoskeletal_1990}, planar knee kinematics from \citet{yamaguchi_planar_1989}, and a lower-limb model with 23 degrees of freedom and 54 musculotendon actuators \citep{arnold_model_2010}. Muscle moment arms, force-generating properties, and functional roles in such models have been characterized extensively \citep{zajac_determining_1989, brand_model_1982, menegaldo_moment_2004, millard_flexing_2013, sherman_what_2013, suderman_moving_2012}, and standard reporting conventions for kinematics and joint mechanics are established by the International Society of Biomechanics \citep{wu_for_1995, wu_isb_2002, wu_isb_2005, derrick_isb_2020}.
-
-The moment arm is the quantity that turns muscle force into joint torque, and two methods of obtaining it anchor the literature. The geometric method computes the arm directly from attachment-point data: the arm about a joint axis is the perpendicular distance from that axis to the muscle's line of action, evaluated at the configuration of interest. \citet{brand_model_1982} assembled the canonical lower-extremity attachment anatomy from cadaver measurements so that such computations could be made at all, and reported that anatomical variation and marking errors rarely shift calculated moment arms by more than twenty percent, usually by less than ten, while attachment data taken from a single unscaled specimen can bias the resulting force predictions substantially. The tendon-excursion method instead defines the arm as the sensitivity of musculotendon length to joint angle, $r = \partial l/\partial\theta$, obtained by perturbing a joint and differencing path lengths. \citet{menegaldo_moment_2004} fitted polynomial regressions of musculotendon lengths and three-dimensional moment arms across the joint space of a lower-limb model so that either quantity could be evaluated cheaply inside large simulations. Both methods deliver configuration-dependent quantities rather than constants, and the dependence compounds for multi-articular muscles, whose arm about one joint varies with the configuration of every joint they cross \citep{zajac_determining_1989, young_analyzing_2019}; the full-muscle rat hindlimb model of \citet{young_analyzing_2019} makes that adjacent-joint dependence a first-class result of its attachment-point technique. OpenSim evaluates moment arms as muscle-length sensitivities of this kind, so the arms used throughout this dissertation are properties of the Gait2392 geometry rather than measured inputs \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2018}.
-
-The human knee is the joint where these definitions are stressed hardest. Its instantaneous center of rotation migrates posteriorly with flexion, a behavior captured by four-bar linkage descriptions of the joint \citep{romero_four-bar_2020, steele_biomimetic_2018}, and \citet{romero_four-bar_2020} treat locating that moving center as a kinematic problem in its own right, solved with natural coordinates so that the answer stays cheap to evaluate when an optimization must recompute it many times. That difficulty is visible at the repository level: the SimTK repository that distributes OpenSim also hosts multiple projects dedicated specifically to the human knee, and they differ in how they formalize joint torques, centers of rotation, and reaction forces for the same anatomy. A hinge knee, a four-bar knee, and a contact-defined tibiofemoral joint give different moment arms for identical muscle paths, so the modeling choice must be matched to the question asked of it. The ISB joint coordinate system recommendations were written against exactly this definitional freedom, to keep reports of joint motion comparable across laboratories \citep{wu_isb_2002}. Throughout this dissertation, Gait2392 serves as the reference against which the robot's muscle routing, moment arms, and torque capabilities are designed and judged.
+Designing a biomimetic leg requires a quantitative benchmark of human muscle--joint behavior. This dissertation uses OpenSim \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2011, seth_opensim_2018} and the Gait2392 model, named for its 23 degrees of freedom and 92 musculotendon actuators. Gait2392 descends from the interactive lower-extremity model of \citet{delp_interactive_1990} and includes musculoskeletal geometry from \citet{hoy_musculoskeletal_1990} and planar knee kinematics from \citet{yamaguchi_planar_1989}; related lower-limb models of the same family are characterized and validated by \citet{arnold_model_2010}. Muscle moment arms, force-generating properties, and functional roles in such models have been characterized extensively \citep{zajac_determining_1989, brand_model_1982, menegaldo_moment_2004, millard_flexing_2013, sherman_what_2013, suderman_moving_2012}, and standard reporting conventions for kinematics and joint mechanics are established by the International Society of Biomechanics \citep{wu_for_1995, wu_isb_2002, wu_isb_2005, derrick_isb_2020}.
+
+Moment arms relate muscle force to joint torque. For a fixed-axis joint, the signed moment arm is $r_{\mathrm{m}}=\hat{\mathbf{a}}\cdot(\mathbf{r}\times\hat{\mathbf{u}})$, where $\hat{\mathbf{a}}$ is the joint-axis direction, $\mathbf{r}$ locates a point on the muscle's line of action relative to the joint, and $\hat{\mathbf{u}}$ is the force direction \citep{brand_model_1982}. The tendon-excursion method instead evaluates the sensitivity of musculotendon length to the joint coordinate, with sign determined by the coordinate and force conventions \citep{menegaldo_moment_2004}. For multiarticular muscles, the result also depends on adjacent-joint configurations \citep{zajac_determining_1989, young_analyzing_2019}. OpenSim uses length sensitivities, so the reference moment arms in this dissertation are properties of the Gait2392-derived geometry \citep{delp_opensim_2007, seth_opensim_2018}. Chapter~\ref{ch:torque} compares the robot's resulting torque predictions with test-stand measurements.
+
+Knee modeling requires particular care because the instantaneous center of rotation changes with flexion. Four-bar descriptions reproduce this behavior through linkage geometry \citep{romero_four-bar_2020, steele_biomimetic_2018}. Gait2392 instead prescribes planar translations as functions of knee flexion \citep{yamaguchi_planar_1989}. These different kinematics produce different moment arms for the same nominal attachment locations. Joint-coordinate conventions must therefore be stated consistently when comparing models and measurements \citep{wu_isb_2002}. Gait2392 provides the human reference in this dissertation, while Steele's four-bar mechanism supplies the physical robot knee.
 
 \section{Biomimetic Knee and Bipedal Robot Model}\label{sec:bg_knee}
 
-Steele's biomimetic knee joint supplies the physical knee used in this dissertation \citep{steele_development_2017, steele_biomimetic_2018, steele_biomimetic_2018-1}. It is a one-degree-of-freedom four-bar linkage whose crossed links reproduce the posterior migration of the human knee's instantaneous center of rotation (ICR) during flexion, so the rolling-and-sliding behavior of the human joint emerges from the link geometry itself rather than from cams or gear profiles; the design was calculated to reproduce an average sliding-to-rolling ratio of 0.079 over a \ang{151} range of motion \citep{steele_biomimetic_2018-1}. Because the ICR migrates, muscle moment arms about the knee change with flexion angle in a human-like way, which is exactly the behavior a torque prediction tool must capture. The biomimetic knee tests of Chapter~\ref{ch:torque} therefore exercise the corrected torque model against this geometry, and Figure~\ref{fig:steele_knee} summarizes the mechanism.
